# Patent Claim Language

## Independent Claim 1

A computer-implemented method for converting a rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory into an optimized polynomial trajectory within a machine tool's travel envelope, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, a rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory defined by a plurality of linear segments and corresponding waypoints representing a tool path for a non-cutting movement of a machine tool between a first position and a second position;

retrieving a travel envelope defining a plurality of physical motion constraints of the machine tool, the physical motion constraints including at least one of maximum axis velocity, maximum axis acceleration, maximum jerk, and workspace boundary limits;

generating, by the one or more processors, an optimized polynomial trajectory by fitting one or more polynomial functions to the plurality of waypoints of the rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory, wherein the fitting comprises constraining the one or more polynomial functions according to the plurality of physical motion constraints of the travel envelope;

evaluating the optimized polynomial trajectory against a three-dimensional model of a workspace comprising at least one obstacle to detect a potential collision between the machine tool and the at least one obstacle along the optimized polynomial trajectory;

upon detecting the potential collision, generating a correction signal identifying a location and a characteristic of the potential collision;

updating, based on the correction signal, at least one of the three-dimensional model of the workspace, the plurality of physical motion constraints, and one or more coefficients of the one or more polynomial functions to produce a corrected trajectory;

iteratively repeating the evaluating, generating a correction signal, and updating steps until the corrected trajectory is determined to be free of potential collisions; and

outputting the corrected trajectory as a collision-free optimized polynomial trajectory for execution by the machine tool.

---

## Dependent Claims

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, wherein the one or more polynomial functions comprise piecewise polynomial spline segments joined at the plurality of waypoints with continuity conditions imposed on position, velocity, and acceleration at each junction between adjacent spline segments.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein fitting the one or more polynomial functions comprises formulating an optimization problem to minimize a cost function representative of at least one of trajectory execution time, energy consumption, and jerk, subject to the plurality of physical motion constraints as inequality constraints.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, wherein evaluating the optimized polynomial trajectory against the three-dimensional model comprises:

discretizing the optimized polynomial trajectory into a plurality of sampled poses each comprising a tool position and orientation;

generating, for each sampled pose, a swept volume representation of the machine tool; and

performing a geometric intersection test between the swept volume representation and the at least one obstacle in the three-dimensional model.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 1, wherein updating the plurality of physical motion constraints comprises reducing at least one of a maximum velocity constraint and a maximum acceleration constraint within a segment of the optimized polynomial trajectory corresponding to the location of the potential collision.

**Claim 6.** The method of claim 1, wherein updating the three-dimensional model of the workspace comprises modifying a boundary of the at least one obstacle based on sensor data received from a sensor disposed proximate the machine tool.

**Claim 7.** The method of claim 1, further comprising, prior to generating the optimized polynomial trajectory, identifying a plurality of critical waypoints from the rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory by removing redundant collinear waypoints, wherein the one or more polynomial functions are fitted to the plurality of critical waypoints.

**Claim 8.** The method of claim 1, wherein the correction signal further identifies a proposed alternative waypoint that circumvents the at least one obstacle, and wherein updating the one or more coefficients of the one or more polynomial functions comprises refitting the one or more polynomial functions to include the proposed alternative waypoint.

**Claim 9.** The method of claim 1, wherein the rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory corresponds to a rapid traverse movement of the machine tool between a tool change position and a machining start position, and wherein the corrected trajectory reduces a cycle time of the rapid traverse movement relative to the rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory.

**Claim 10.** The method of claim 1, wherein the travel envelope is defined for a plurality of controllable axes of the machine tool, and wherein constraining the one or more polynomial functions comprises independently constraining a polynomial function corresponding to each of the plurality of controllable axes.

---

## Independent Claim 11 (System Claim)

A system for converting a rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory into an optimized polynomial trajectory within a machine tool's travel envelope, the system comprising:

one or more processors; and

a non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the system to:

receive a rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory defined by a plurality of linear segments and corresponding waypoints;

retrieve a travel envelope defining a plurality of physical motion constraints of a machine tool;

generate an optimized polynomial trajectory by fitting one or more polynomial functions to the plurality of waypoints, constrained according to the plurality of physical motion constraints;

evaluate the optimized polynomial trajectory against a three-dimensional model of a workspace to detect a potential collision;

generate, in response to detecting the potential collision, a correction signal;

update, based on the correction signal, at least one of the three-dimensional model, the plurality of physical motion constraints, and the one or more polynomial functions to produce a corrected trajectory;

iteratively repeat the evaluation, correction signal generation, and update operations until the corrected trajectory is free of potential collisions; and

output the corrected trajectory for execution by the machine tool.

---

## Independent Claim 12 (CRM Claim)

A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:

receiving a rectilinear non-productive tool trajectory comprising a plurality of waypoints;

retrieving a travel envelope defining physical motion constraints of a machine tool;

fitting one or more polynomial functions to the plurality of waypoints subject to the physical motion constraints to generate an optimized polynomial trajectory;

evaluating the optimized polynomial trajectory against a three-dimensional model of a workspace to detect a potential collision between the machine tool and an obstacle;

in response to detecting the potential collision, generating a correction signal and updating, based on the correction signal, at least one of the three-dimensional model, the physical motion constraints, and the one or more polynomial functions to produce a corrected trajectory;

repeating the evaluating and updating operations in a feedback loop until the corrected trajectory is collision-free; and

outputting the corrected trajectory for execution by the machine tool.
